  "text": "« neutral language: when gathering feedback or presenting options, use\nneutral language that doesn’t lead respondents toward a particular choice.\nfor instance, rather than asking users if they \"love\" a new feature, ask how\nthey \"feel\" about it to get honest feedback.\n\n+ gather more context: acknowledge when you lack enough data to make an\ninformed decision and seek additional information to better understand the\nsituation.\n\n( wn saved | ( ~> share w altutor [mg rf\nframing bias happens when people make choices based on how\ninformation is shown, not the facts themselves. to avoid this, you can do\na few things:\n\nfirst, look at information from different angles. for example, if 90% of\npeople like something, think about the 10% who don't. second, ask\ndifferent people for their opinions to get a wider view. third, use simple\nand fair words when asking for feedback, like asking how someone feels\ninstead of if they love something. lastly, if you don't have enough\ninformation, look for more to help you decide better.\n\nthanks for your feedback!\n\n@ simplify v ) @ al tutor preferences\n",
